    "body": "The term 'Fauna' is derived from the Roman goddess Fauna, the sister of Faunus (the god of the forest, plains, and fields) and the Roman goddess of earth and fertility. It refers to the species of animals in a region."
  },
  {
    "block_id": "BLK_CH10_04",
    "type": "history",
    "title": "Cultural Integration: Forests in Ancient India",
    "content": "In ancient India, forests (Aranya) were deeply respected. The 'Aranyakas' (forest books) are a part of the Vedas, composed by sages living in forests. They symbolize a deep spiritual connection with nature, emphasizing that humans are a part of the ecosystem, not its masters.",
    "universal_block_metadata": {
      "difficulty": "Intermediate",
      "estimated_minutes": 5,
      "bloom": "Analyze"
    },
    "id": "KOBJ_CLAS_SOCI_SCIE_CH10_HIST_004",
    "title_hi": "Cultural Integration: Forests in Ancient India",
    "title_gu": "Cultural Integration: Forests in Ancient India",
    "body": "In ancient India, forests (Aranya) were deeply respected. The 'Aranyakas' (forest books) are a part of the Vedas, composed by sages living in forests. They symbolize a deep spiritual connection with nature, emphasizing that humans are a part of the ecosystem, not its masters."
  },
